    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D3"],
      "pins": ["D3.4 (DIN)"],
      "nets": ["NET0004"],
      "observed": "On the Microcontroller sheet (mcu.kicad_sch), the WS2812B status LED D3 has its data input pin 4 (DIN) labelled NET0004, but the net name NET0004 appears nowhere on any pin of U8 (ESP32-C3-MINI-1). The only net boxes on U8 are NET0011/NET0017 (left), NET0007/NET0012 (USB), NET0008/NET0001/NET0005/NET0003 (SPI), NET0006 (SO), plus TP2/TP4 and the fan-tach protection nets NET0002/NET0018. Thus D3.DIN/NET0004 is a floating net with no driver.",
      "expected": "The specification requires the onboard WS2812B status LED to be driven by the MCU (TL;DR: 'a single onboard WS2812B status LED (D1)'; firmware/src/led.rs sets its colour per run state), so D3.DIN must be connected to one of the ESP32-C3 GPIOs.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: D3 WS2812B, pin 4 DIN -> NET0004, pin 1 VDD -> +3V3, pin 3 VSS -> GND, pin 2 DOUT -> NC; NET0004 not present on any U8 pin"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Exposes status over Wi-Fi via a small webserver ... and a single onboard WS2812B status LED (D1)."}
      ],
      "impact": "The status LED has no data input tied to the microcontroller, so the firmware's per-state colour output never reaches the WS2812B; the onboard status indication is lost (LED stays off / at its power-on default).",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The power, heater, thermocouple-SPI and USB paths trace consistently against the specification, but the WS2812B status LED's data line (NET0004 on D3.DIN) is not connected to any ESP32-C3 GPIO pin on the Microcontroller sheet, leaving the status LED undriven - an open-connection deviation from the intended design."
}

